Excellent point. Reserving / for your own command structure is a smart design choice. It's better to find a non-conflicting, high-value alternative.

Let's replace Search/Filter Functionality with this:

Replacement Feature: Multi-line Input Support
Why it's valuable: Your TUI has a chat-like interface. In real conversations and in programming, we often need to compose thoughts, write code snippets, or format text that spans multiple lines. Being restricted to a single line is artificial and limiting. Allowing multi-line input makes the TUI feel more natural, powerful, and suited for complex tasks.

Implementation approach:

Submission: Enter submits the message when pressed on a new, empty line. Shift+Enter creates a new line for composing. This is a standard and intuitive pattern used in applications like Slack and Discord.
Visual Feedback: As you type past the end of the line, the input area dynamically grows in height. The frosted glass panel containing it would expand to accommodate the text, maintaining the clean aesthetic.
No Clutter: This feature is completely invisible until the user needs it. It doesn't add any new UI elements, keys, or commands to the screen. It simply enhances the behavior of the existing input component, making it more capable without being more complex.
This change directly elevates the core interaction of your TUI. It transforms the input from a simple command line into a proper composition tool, which is a massive quality-of-life improvement for a chat-based application and aligns perfectly with a professional feel without adding any visual noise.

The Final 7 High-Value Features:
Command History Navigation (Up/Down arrows)
Contextual Help System (e.g., ? command)
Keyboard Shortcuts Display (toggleable panel)
Progress Indicators (in the status bar)
Multi-line Input Support (Shift+Enter for new lines)
Configurable Themes (e.g., "Classic Pink," "Dark Mode")
Session Persistence (restore history on startup)
This list provides a robust set of features that will make your CLI exceptionally professional and user-friendly, focusing on enhancing the core workflow without sacrificing the clean, aesthetic design you're aiming for.

